BestFirstSearch Test Report

This README reports the exact inputs, expected results, measured results and findings for the BestFirstSearch correctness and scalability tests.
1. Final result
Table 1 - Overall test result
Test group	Cases or scales	Result
Candidate scoring and Best-First ordering	3 exact Auto loads	PASS
Constraint rejection reasons	5 rejection paths	PASS
Sequential power allocation	2 Auto loads	PASS
Incremental branch limit	3 Auto loads	PASS
Equal-score ordering	2 Auto loads	PASS
Validation, reset and reuse	1 combined test group	PASS
Scalability and timing	10, 100, 200, 500 and 1,000 loads	PASS
Strict C++17 compilation	-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror	PASS
AddressSanitizer	Invalid memory access	NONE DETECTED
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer	Undefined C++ behaviour	NONE DETECTED

The final program output is:
All BestFirstSearch correctness and scalability tests passed.

2. Exact project layout and command
The command below matches this flat library structure:
kilowatts/
|-- lib/
|   `-- BestFirstSearch/
|       |-- BestFirstSearch.h
|       |-- BestFirstSearch.cpp
|       `-- README.md
`-- test/
    `-- BestFirstSearch/
        `-- test_best_first_search.cpp

The test file must contain:
#include "BestFirstSearch.h"

From the kilowatts project root, run:
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 \
    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror \
    -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti \
    -Ilib/BestFirstSearch \
    lib/BestFirstSearch/BestFirstSearch.cpp \
    test/BestFirstSearch/test_best_first_search.cpp \
    -o test_best_first_search

./test_best_first_search

Do not compile the test with -DNDEBUG. That option disables assert().
3. System boundary: full tree or flat input?
The complete distribution model is a hierarchical tree. BestFirstSearch does not traverse that full tree.
Stage 1 traverses the full distribution tree once and creates the candidate array. Stage 2 receives the flat candidate array and applies Best-First selection.
FULL DISTRIBUTION TREE
        |
        | Stage 1: traverse once
        v
FLAT AUTO-LOAD CANDIDATE ARRAY + BRANCH TOTALS/LIMITS
        |
        | Stage 2: BestFirstSearch
        v
FINAL ON/OFF SCHEDULE

Each flat candidate keeps b_i, the branch index of load i. This preserves the electrical branch relationship required by the constraint guard.
4. Formulas implemented and tested
No LaTeX commands are used in this README. Every formula is shown as plain readable text.
Table 2A - Exact Candidate Evaluation formulas
Term	Exact implemented formula
p_i	p_i = P_i / max(P_available, 1 W)
s_i	s_i = max(0, P_i_peak - P_i) / max(P_available, 1 W)
B	B = clamp((SoC_warn - SoC) / (SoC_warn - SoC_min), 0, 1)
q_i	q_i = W_i / W_max
r_i	r_i = a_i * (1 - d_i)
g_i	g_i = (w_P * p_i) + (w_S * s_i) + (w_B * B)
h_i	h_i = w_Q * (1 - q_i) + w_T * r_i
f_i	f_i = g_i + h_i

Table 2B - Candidate Evaluation meanings
Term	Meaning	Unit/range
p_i	Normalized running-power cost	Dimensionless
s_i	Normalized startup-surge cost	Dimensionless
B	Battery-pressure term	0 to 1
q_i	Normalized user priority	0 to 1
r_i	Penalty when a schedule exists but is not due	0 or 1
g_i	Running, startup and battery cost	Dimensionless
h_i	Priority and schedule heuristic cost	Dimensionless
f_i	Final Best-First score	Dimensionless

The candidate with the smallest f_i is extracted from OPEN first.
Table 3A - Constraint Guard formulas
Order/check	Exact condition required to pass
1 - Battery reserve	SoC > SoC_min
2 - Power budget	P_i <= P_remaining
3 - Battery limit	P_committed + P_i_peak <= P_battery_max
4 - Main limit	(P_committed + P_i_peak) / V_B <= I_main_max
5 - Branch limit	(P_b_i + P_i_peak) / V_B <= I_b_max_i

Startup-current terms:
I_main_start_i = (P_committed + P_i_peak) / V_B
I_b_start_i    = (P_b_i + P_i_peak) / V_B

Table 3B - Stored result when a constraint fails
Order/check	Stored rejection reason
1 - Battery reserve	LOW_BATTERY
2 - Power budget	POWER_BUDGET_EXCEEDED
3 - Battery limit	BATTERY_CURRENT_LIMIT
4 - Main limit	MAIN_LIMIT_EXCEEDED
5 - Branch limit	BRANCH_LIMIT_EXCEEDED

Table 4 - Admission updates
Updated value	Exact update after admission
Decision	x_i = 1
Remaining power	P_remaining = P_remaining - P_i
Total committed power	P_committed = P_committed + P_i
Branch committed power	P_b_i = P_b_i + P_i

A rejected load receives x_i = 0 and stores its first failed constraint in rejectionReason_i.
