8. Scalability workload tested
The scalability test uses deterministic values, so every execution receives the same candidate data.
Table 13 - Candidate-generation formulas
Candidate value	Test formula
Load ID	autoLoadId_i = i + 1
Branch	b_i = 0
Running power	P_i = 1.0 + ((i * 37) mod 10) * 0.05 W
Peak power	P_i_peak = P_i + 0.5 + ((i * 17) mod 5) * 0.05 W
Priority	W_i = ((i * 31) mod 100) + 1
Has schedule	a_i = 1 when i mod 3 = 0; otherwise 0
Schedule due	d_i = 1 when i mod 6 = 0; otherwise 0

Candidate ranges were P_i = 1.00-1.45 W and P_i_peak = 1.50-2.15 W.
Table 14A - Scalability battery and power inputs
Candidate loads n	SoC	P_available	Initial P_remaining	P_battery_max
10	80%	20 W	20 W	40 W
100	80%	200 W	200 W	400 W
200	80%	400 W	400 W	800 W
500	80%	1,000 W	1,000 W	2,000 W
1,000	80%	2,000 W	2,000 W	4,000 W

Table 14B - Scalability electrical-limit inputs
Candidate loads n	Branch used	V_B	I_main_max	I_b_max_i
10	0	12 V	20 A	20 A
100	0	12 V	200 A	200 A
200	0	12 V	400 A	400 A
500	0	12 V	1,000 A	1,000 A
1,000	0	12 V	2,000 A	2,000 A

These are synthetic algorithm-scaling limits. They are not proposed physical breaker or battery ratings.
For each load count, the test performs 10 unmeasured warm-up cycles followed by 101 measured cycles. The reported time is the median of the 101 measured cycles.
