9. Execution-time findings
Reference host:
Compiler: g++ 13.3.0
Build:    C++17 with -O2, exceptions OFF, RTTI OFF
CPU:      AMD EPYC 9V74 virtual CPU
System:   64-bit Linux

The values below are medians from three complete benchmark executions. Each execution internally used the median of 101 measured cycles after 10 warm-up cycles.
Table 17 - Measured host execution time
Candidate loads	Setup time	Registration time	Scheduler run() time	Complete time	Result
10	0.22 us	0.18 us	0.42 us	0.86 us	PASS
100	0.24 us	2.37 us	5.50 us	8.10 us	PASS
200	0.28 us	6.13 us	11.69 us	18.14 us	PASS
500	0.40 us	31.27 us	32.42 us	64.08 us	PASS
1,000	0.43 us	108.67 us	77.69 us	186.60 us	PASS

us means microseconds. These host times will differ on another computer. They are not ESP32 timing measurements.
Table 18 - Normalized O(n log n) timing evidence
n	n * log2(n)	Scheduler time	time / (n * log2(n))
10	33.22	0.42 us	0.01264 us
100	664.39	5.50 us	0.00828 us
200	1,528.77	11.69 us	0.00765 us
500	4,482.89	32.42 us	0.00723 us
1,000	9,965.78	77.69 us	0.00780 us

The normalized value becomes approximately stable from 100 through 1,000 candidates. This supports the source-code complexity analysis but does not replace the mathematical proof.
10. Complexity finding
Table 19 - BestFirstSearch::run() complexity
Operation inside run()	Number of operations	Cost per operation	Total cost
Evaluate candidate formulas	n	O(1)	O(n)
Insert candidates into binary min-heap OPEN	n	O(log n)	O(n log n)
Extract candidates from OPEN	n	O(log n)	O(n log n)
Constraint check and decision update	n	O(1)	O(n)

Therefore:
T_run(n)
  = O(n) + O(n log n) + O(n log n) + O(n)
  = O(n log n)

Memory = O(n)

Table 20 - Complete current path complexity
Path	Current complexity	Reason
Full-tree traversal in Stage 1	O(number of tree nodes)	Each tree node is visited once
Repeated addAutoLoad() registration	Worst-case O(n^2)	Duplicate IDs are checked with a linear scan
BestFirstSearch::run()	O(n log n)	Binary min-heap insertions and extractions

The honest conclusion is that run() is O(n log n), while the current complete registration-plus-scheduling path is worst-case O(n^2) until duplicate-ID lookup is optimized.
11. Complete term glossary
Table 21 - Input and configuration terms
Term	Exact meaning	Unit/range
i	Candidate-array position, not the load ID	0 to n - 1
n	Number of candidate Auto loads	Count
autoLoadId_i	Unique system ID of Auto load i	Unsigned integer
b_i	Zero-based branch index belonging to load i	Integer, no unit
P_i	Normal running power of load i	Watts
P_i_peak	Peak startup power of load i	Watts
W_i	User priority of load i; it is not watts	0 to W_max
W_max	Maximum permitted priority	Positive integer
a_i	Whether load i has a schedule	0 or 1
d_i	Whether the schedule is currently due	0 or 1
SoC	Current battery state of charge	0% to 100%
SoC_min	Protected minimum battery state of charge	Percent
SoC_warn	Battery warning threshold	Percent
P_available	Total safe power offered to the scheduler	Watts
P_remaining	Power not yet assigned to admitted Auto loads	Watts
P_committed	Running power already committed	Watts
P_battery_max	Maximum permitted battery power	Watts
V_B	Battery/DC-bus voltage	Volts
I_main_max	Maximum main-path current	Amperes
P_b_i	Running power already committed to branch b_i; not the power of load i	Watts
I_b_max_i	Maximum current of branch b_i	Amperes
w_P, w_S, w_B, w_Q, w_T	Non-negative scoring weights	Dimensionless

Table 22 - Calculated and output terms
Term	Exact meaning	Unit/range
p_i	Normalized running-power term	Dimensionless
s_i	Normalized startup-surge term	Dimensionless
B	Battery-pressure term	0 to 1
q_i	Normalized user-priority term	0 to 1
r_i	Schedule-penalty term	0 or 1
g_i	Running, startup and battery cost	Dimensionless
h_i	Priority and schedule heuristic cost	Dimensionless
f_i	Final Best-First score	Dimensionless
I_main_start_i	Predicted main startup current for candidate i	Amperes
I_b_start_i	Predicted startup current on branch b_i	Amperes
x_i	Final decision	1 = ON, 0 = OFF
OPEN	Binary min-heap ordered by the smallest f_i	Up to n indices
processingOrder_i	Position in which candidate i was extracted	0 to n - 1
rejectionReason_i	First constraint that rejected candidate i	Rejection code

Table 23 - Rejection codes
Code	Meaning
NONE	Candidate passed all constraints
LOW_BATTERY	SoC is at or below SoC_min
POWER_BUDGET_EXCEEDED	P_i is greater than P_remaining
BATTERY_CURRENT_LIMIT	Battery startup-power limit would be exceeded
MAIN_LIMIT_EXCEEDED	Main startup-current limit would be exceeded
BRANCH_LIMIT_EXCEEDED	Branch startup-current limit would be exceeded

12. What this test does not claim
Table 24 - Remaining integration and hardware tests
Not covered by this host test	Required later
Full distribution-tree traversal	Stage 1 unit and integration test
INA219 voltage/current/power	Sensor calibration and hardware test
Battery SoC accuracy	Battery model and discharge test
ESP32 execution time	Measure using the ESP-IDF high-resolution timer
Relay operation	GPIO, relay and feedback test
ESP-NOW communication	Central-node/smart-node integration test
Long-term memory stability	Endurance test
Real DC electrical limits	Controlled hardware safety test

13. Final finding
The correctness suite passed every formula, ordering, rejection, capacity-update, branch-update, tie-handling, validation and reset test. The scalability suite passed every size from 10 to 1,000 candidates, with every score finite, every candidate processed exactly once, every processing position unique and every final power total correct.
The implementation of BestFirstSearch::run() achieves the proposed O(n log n) scheduling complexity. The current duplicate-ID scan in addAutoLoad() remains a separately documented O(n^2) registration limitation.
